 ## Overview
-This method enables GPU volume rendering of large, sparse volumetric datasets by combining offline fixed-rate lossy compression on the CPU (using OpenVDB) with fast, low-overhead on-the-fly decompression and sampling on the GPU (using NanoVDB) [S2]. It solves the problem of rendering volumes that exceed single-GPU memory capacity by significantly reducing the memory footprint while maintaining random access patterns suitable for incoherent workloads like path tracing [S2]. Unlike hierarchical space-skipping approaches which show diminishing returns with RGBA transfer functions, this method typically employs a uniform majorant grid for traversal acceleration to facilitate direct comparison with dense texture rendering [S1, S2].
+This method enables GPU volume rendering of large, sparse volumetric datasets by combining offline fixed-rate lossy compression on the CPU (using OpenVDB) with fast, low-overhead on-the-fly decompression and sampling on the GPU (using NanoVDB) [S2]. It solves the problem of rendering volumes that exceed single-GPU memory capacity by significantly reducing the memory footprint while maintaining random access patterns suitable for incoherent workloads like path tracing [S2]. Unlike hierarchical space-skipping approaches which show diminishing returns with RGBA transfer functions, this method employs a uniform majorant grid for traversal acceleration to facilitate direct comparison with dense texture rendering [S1, S2].
 
